# 5. Customers Module

## Module description

This module registers frequent customers for:

* Personalized prices
* Discounts
* Customer credit
* Quotation delivery
* Marketing communication
* Push notifications
* Purchase history
* Reports

The normalized table name should be `customers` rather than `clients`.

---

## 5.1 Table: `customers`

### Description

Stores individuals and business customers.

### Columns

```text
id                          BIGINT UNSIGNED PRIMARY KEY AUTO_INCREMENT
business_id                 BIGINT UNSIGNED NOT NULL
code                        VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL
customer_type               VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL DEFAULT 'individual'
first_name                  VARCHAR(80) NULL
last_name                   VARCHAR(100) NULL
business_name               VARCHAR(160) NULL
tax_id                      VARCHAR(30) NULL
email                       VARCHAR(254) NULL
phone                       VARCHAR(20) NULL
notes                       TEXT NULL
email_marketing_consent     BOOLEAN NOT NULL DEFAULT FALSE
push_marketing_consent      BOOLEAN NOT NULL DEFAULT FALSE
consent_updated_at          TIMESTAMP NULL
is_active                   BOOLEAN NOT NULL DEFAULT TRUE
created_at                  TIMESTAMP NULL
updated_at                  TIMESTAMP NULL
deleted_at                  TIMESTAMP NULL
```

### Relationships

```text
business_id -> businesses.id
```

### Allowed customer types

```text
individual
business
```

### Constraints and indexes

```text
UNIQUE(business_id, code)
INDEX(business_id, phone)
INDEX(business_id, email)
INDEX(business_id, tax_id)
INDEX(business_id, last_name, first_name)
INDEX(business_id, business_name)
INDEX(business_id, is_active)
```

### Business rules

* Email and phone may both be nullable at the database level.
* Application validation should require at least one contact method when needed.
* An individual should have a first name.
* A business customer should have a business name.
* Marketing communication requires explicit consent.

---

## 5.2 Table: `customer_addresses`

### Description

Stores one or more addresses for each customer.

### Columns

```text
id                  BIGINT UNSIGNED PRIMARY KEY AUTO_INCREMENT
customer_id         BIGINT UNSIGNED NOT NULL
type                VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL DEFAULT 'primary'
address_line_1      VARCHAR(160) NOT NULL
address_line_2      VARCHAR(160) NULL
neighborhood        VARCHAR(100) NULL
city                VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL
state               VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL
postal_code         VARCHAR(12) NOT NULL
country_code        CHAR(2) NOT NULL DEFAULT 'MX'
is_primary          BOOLEAN NOT NULL DEFAULT FALSE
created_at          TIMESTAMP NULL
updated_at          TIMESTAMP NULL
```

### Relationships

```text
customer_id -> customers.id
```

### Indexes

```text
INDEX(customer_id, is_primary)
INDEX(customer_id, type)
```

---

## 5.3 Table: `customer_devices`

### Description

Stores customer devices and Firebase Cloud Messaging tokens.

An FCM token must not be stored directly on the customer because:

* A customer may own multiple devices.
* Tokens may rotate.
* Tokens may be revoked.
* Tokens may change when the application is reinstalled.

### Columns

```text
id                  BIGINT UNSIGNED PRIMARY KEY AUTO_INCREMENT
customer_id         BIGINT UNSIGNED NOT NULL
token               TEXT NOT NULL
token_hash          CHAR(64) NOT NULL
platform            VARCHAR(20) NULL
last_seen_at        TIMESTAMP NULL
revoked_at          TIMESTAMP NULL
created_at          TIMESTAMP NULL
updated_at          TIMESTAMP NULL
```

### Relationships

```text
customer_id -> customers.id
```

### Constraints and indexes

```text
UNIQUE(token_hash)
INDEX(customer_id, revoked_at)
INDEX(customer_id, last_seen_at)
```

### Business rules

* Generate `token_hash` using SHA-256.
* Use the hash for uniqueness and indexed lookups.
* Do not attempt to index the complete `TEXT` token.

