    targetElement: this.targetElement,
    params: params,
    getParam: function(param) {
        if (typeof params[param] !== "undefined") {
            return params[param];
        }
        return null;
    },
    hasParam: function(param) {
        return typeof params[param] !== "undefined"
    }
```

The data-jscontroller attribute is not necessary to add it to the element containing the event, it can be added to a parent element, like the following example:

```html
<div data-jscontroller="NotificationsMenu>
    <button type="button"  data-jsclick="@open" data-jsparams="{'notifications_menu_id' : 'notifications_menu'}">Open menu</button>

    <button type="button"  data-jsclick="@close" data-jsparams="{'notifications_menu_id' : 'notifications_menu'}">Close menu</button>
</div>
```

### Usage of modules SubmitNativeEvent and JSFormController

JSFormController - Form Submission Lifecycle

#### Overview

JSFormController is a client-side form orchestration layer responsible for:

* Form validation
* Controller method dispatching
* Payload extraction
* Asynchronous submission
* Event lifecycle management
* Response handling

The controller acts as a bridge between HTML forms and JavaScript controller modules.

#### Form Declaration

A form becomes managed by JSFormController when it contains:

<form
    data-jscontroller="MyController"
    data-jsinstance="true"
    data-on-beforesubmit="@onBeforeSubmit"
    data-on-success="@onSuccess"
    data-on-error="@onError" 
    onsubmit="new(requireModule('SubmitNativeEvent'))(event, this); return false;">
</form>

The form submission is intercepted through:

onsubmit="new(requireModule('SubmitNativeEvent'))(event, this, 'async'); return false;"

#### Submission Flow
1. Native Submit Event

The browser dispatches the native submit event.

The SubmitNativeEvent module intercepts the event and prevents the browser's default page reload behavior. it also has tree modes, 'async', 'sync', 'validate'.

event.preventDefault();

The form element is then passed to JSFormController.

2. Controller Resolution

JSFormController reads:

data-jscontroller="MyController"

and loads the corresponding module:

requireModule("MyController");
3. Controller Instantiation

If:

data-jsinstance="true"

is present, a new controller instance is created for the current submission.

Example:

new MyController(event, controllerContext);

Where:

controllerContext = {
    form: HTMLFormElement,
    submitter: HTMLElement,
    payload: FormData
}

Each submission receives its own isolated controller instance.

No controller state is persisted between submissions unless explicitly stored elsewhere.

4. Payload Extraction

The form data is serialized into:

FormData

Example:

var payload = new FormData(form);

The payload contains:

payload.get(name);
payload.set(name, value);
payload.has(name);
payload.delete(name);

and preserves file uploads.

5. Validation Phase

The controller iterates through all form fields.

Fields containing:

data-jsvalidator="@validateName"

are mapped to controller methods:

controller.validateName(target, validationContext);

Example:

<input
    name="fullname"
    data-jsvalidator="@validateName">

invokes:

validateName(field, ctx);
Validation Context

A validation context object is generated.

Example:

{
    valid: false,
    value: "",
    errors: {
        required: true,
        minlength: false,
        maxlength: false,
        pattern: false,
        typeMismatch: false,
        customError: false
    }
}

This object abstracts browser validation rules.

6. Submission Cancellation

If any field is invalid:

ctx.valid === false

submission is aborted.

No network request is sent.

The validation callbacks remain responsible for displaying UI errors.

7. Before Submit Hook

If validation succeeds:

data-on-beforesubmit="@onBeforeSubmit"

is resolved.

The controller method is invoked:

controller.onBeforeSubmit(payload);

Typical responsibilities:

Disable submit buttons
Show loading indicators
Create optimistic UI placeholders
Modify payload data
8. Request Construction

The form attributes are converted into an AsyncRequest.

Example:

new AsyncRequest(form.action)

The request builder extracts:

method
action
headers
payload
files

and prepares the underlying XMLHttpRequest.

9. Request Dispatch

The request is sent asynchronously.

xhr.send(payload);

The payload may be:

FormData
QueryString
JSON
Raw text

depending on the request configuration.

10. Progress Events

When uploads exist:

request.withFiles === true

upload events are attached:

xhr.upload

Supported events:

loadstart
progress
load
loadend
abort
error
timeout

Progress callbacks receive:

{
    loaded: Number,
    total: Number,
    percent: Number,
    timeStamp: Number
}

Where:

percent = Math.min(
    100,
    Math.ceil((loaded / total) * 100)
);
11. Response Handling

When:

xhr.readyState === XMLHttpRequest.DONE

the response is evaluated.

Success Responses

Status:

200

invokes:

controller.onSuccess(payload, xhr);

The original payload is preserved and passed back alongside the response.

Error Responses

Statuses:

400
401
403
404
422
500

invoke:

controller.onError(xhr);

or the configured error callback.

12. Post Submission State

After success or failure, the controller is expected to:

Re-enable UI controls
Remove loading states
Update rendered content
Display notifications

The framework itself does not automatically modify UI state.

#### Controller Responsibilities

A controller may implement:

validateField(...)
onBeforeSubmit(...)
onProgress(...)
onSuccess(...)
onError(...)
onAbort(...)
onPostSubmit(...)

All methods are optional.

Methods are discovered dynamically using the form's data attributes.

#### State Model

Controllers are instantiated per submission.

Example:

new Controller(...)

This means:

* State is isolated
* No memory leaks between submissions
* Concurrent submissions do not share state

Any persistent state must be stored explicitly in:

* Global modules
* Singletons
* Dedicated stores
* DOM state
* Browser storage

#### Architectural Pattern

The system follows a hybrid pattern combining:

* MVC controller dispatch
* Event-driven callbacks
* FormData serialization
* XMLHttpRequest transport
* Declarative HTML bindings

The form defines behavior through attributes while controller modules provide the execution logic.

